            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Makes values from start to stop that grow by the same RATIO at every step: a geometric sequence. np.geomspace 220 1760 4 is 220 440 880 1760 - each double the last: the A of four octaves.\n\nEqual ratios are how pitch, loudness, brightness and many other perceptions work, so a control or a set of bands spaced this way feels even, where equal steps (np.linspace) feel crowded at one end. Use it for musical intervals, octave or third-octave bands, a range of time scales, zoom levels, or delays that grow by a fixed factor.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.geomspace <start> <stop> <steps>\nDefaults: 1, 1000, 4 steps.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.geomspace 20 20000 11\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes the values and sends them.\n\nstart, stop (float boxes, also inlets):\nThe first and last values, both included. Neither can be 0, and both must be on the same side of zero (a ratio cannot take you across it).\n\nsteps (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many values.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ngeomspace out:\nThe values.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nMade at load: four octaves of A from 220 Hz, 220 440 880 1760; and 1 to 1000 in four steps, 1 10 100 1000. The 880 shows as 880.0000000000001: values reached by multiplying ratios carry a little rounding in their last digits. It makes no audible or visible difference; put round after the node if you need whole numbers.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.logspace takes exponents instead of end values. np.linspace spaces by equal amounts. log turns equal ratios back into equal steps.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Makes values from start to stop that grow by the same RATIO at every step: a geometric sequence. np.geomspace 220 1760 4 is 220 440 880 1760 - each double the last: the A of four octaves.\n\nEqual ratios are how pitch, loudness, brightness and many other perceptions work, so a control or a set of bands spaced this way feels even, where equal steps (np.linspace) feel crowded at one end. Use it for musical intervals, octave or third-octave bands, a range of time scales, zoom levels, or delays that grow by a fixed factor.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.geomspace <start> <stop> <steps>\nDefaults: 1, 1000, 4 steps.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.geomspace 20 20000 11\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\n(button):\nMakes the values and sends them.\n\nstart, stop (float boxes, also inlets):\nThe first and last values, both included. Neither can be 0, and both must be on the same side of zero (a ratio cannot take you across it).\n\nsteps (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many values.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ngeomspace out:\nThe values.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nMade at load: four octaves of A from 220 Hz, 220 440 880 1760; and 1 to 1000 in four steps, 1 10 100 1000. The 880 shows as 880.0000000000001: values reached by multiplying ratios carry a little rounding in their last digits. It makes no audible or visible difference; put round after the node if you need whole numbers.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.logspace takes exponents instead of end values. np.linspace spaces by equal amounts. log turns equal ratios back into equal steps."
        },
